      answerMd: `
# String Compression

## 🧠 The Core Intuition

You're doing a run-length encoding: replace consecutive identical characters with the character followed by its count. The only subtlety is knowing *when a run ends* — it ends when the next character is different (or you hit the end of the string).

> **Mental model:** You're scanning a conveyor belt of letters. You pick up the first letter and start a tally. Keep tallying as long as the same letter keeps coming. The moment a different letter arrives, write down what you've been counting and start a new tally. This is naturally an "inner while" inside an "outer while."

\`\`\`
"aabcccdddd"
i=0: char='a', count consecutive a's → 2  → append "a2"
i=2: char='b', count consecutive b's → 1  → append "b1"
i=3: char='c', count consecutive c's → 3  → append "c3"
i=6: char='d', count consecutive d's → 4  → append "d4"
result = "a2b1c3d4" ✅ (shorter than original → return it)
\`\`\`

\`\`\`java
String compress(String s) {
    StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
    int i = 0;

    while (i < s.length()) {
        char current = s.charAt(i);
        int count = 0;

        while (i < s.length() && s.charAt(i) == current) {
            i++; count++;           // consume the run
        }
        sb.append(current).append(count);
    }

    String result = sb.toString();
    return result.length() < s.length() ? result : s; // only compress if it helps
}
\`\`\`

> 💡 **The "only return compressed if shorter" rule is easy to miss under pressure.** If the string is "abc", compression gives "a1b1c1" — longer. Always compare lengths and return the original if compression makes it worse.

---

## 🔑 In-Place Compression (LeetCode 443 variant)

Modify the char array in-place, return the new length. Use a write pointer:

\`\`\`java
int compress(char[] chars) {
    int write = 0, i = 0;
    while (i < chars.length) {
        char curr = chars[i];
        int count = 0;
        while (i < chars.length && chars[i] == curr) { i++; count++; }
        chars[write++] = curr;
        if (count > 1)
            for (char c : Integer.toString(count).toCharArray())
                chars[write++] = c;
    }
    return write;
}
\`\`\`

> 💡 Multi-digit counts (e.g., 12 consecutive a's → "a12") need to be written digit by digit. \`Integer.toString(count).toCharArray()\` handles this cleanly.

---

## ⏱️ Complexity
| What | Value |
|------|-------|
| Time | O(n) |
| Space | O(n) for StringBuilder; O(1) for in-place variant |

---

## 🧠 Tips & Tricks
- The outer \`while (i < s.length())\` advances \`i\` inside the inner loop — don't increment \`i\` again in the outer loop or you'll skip characters.
- **Decompression** (reverse this): scan char-by-char, when you hit a digit accumulate it, when you hit a letter repeat it count times. Same "run detection" idea, reversed.
- This pattern (outer loop picks a character, inner loop counts consecutive occurrences) appears in many string problems. Burn it into muscle memory.
`
